# R intermediates (may contain per-patient simulated rows — keep out of git).
# output/preview/ holds smoke-test exports and is intermediate for the same reason.
output/
logs/
.Rhistory
.RData
.Rproj.user/

# docs/ is NOT ignored -- it is the published site and is tracked in full. It
# needs no negation here: `output/` above does not match it. (Two `!docs/...`
# lines used to sit here negating patterns that never matched, which read as
# though docs/ were ignored and rescued.) The exported web artifacts are
# aggregate and safe to publish; the per-patient intermediates under output/ are
# what stays out.

# Internal editorial notes — not part of the published repo
CAST_Trajectory_Covariance_Notes_*.docx
CAST_Trajectory_Covariance_Notes_*.md
CAST_Trajectory_Covariance_Notes_*.patch

# Reviewer comment documents. The review itself is transcribed and answered in
# development/, which is what belongs in the repo; the reviewer's own file is a
# private working document and is not ours to publish.
*comments.docx
*comments.doc
*_review.docx

# Screenshots and other local capture artifacts: large, regenerable, and not
# part of the published site.
CAST_demo_screenshot.pdf
*_screenshot.pdf

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
*~

# Publisher / paywalled reference PDFs are NEVER committed. Committing is
# publishing, and a private repository can be shared or made public later, by
# which point the blob is already in the history and removing it needs a rewrite
# of every commit rather than a delete. Keep local copies outside the repository
# and cite the DOI in References/README.md.
References/*.pdf
References/*.PDF
!References/README.md

# Build artifacts of files that ARE tracked as markdown. The .md is the source
# and is committed; the .docx is a convenience conversion for reading in Word and
# is regenerated on demand, so committing it would put two copies of the same
# prose in the history and let them disagree.
development/*.docx

# Written by stale_constant_audit.py on every clean --strict run and removed by
# any failing one. It binds to a sha256 of constant_registry.yaml, so a committed
# copy would go stale the moment the registry is edited and would then read as an
# attestation that no longer attests to anything. The routing record in
# audit_manifest.yaml is where that verdict is kept.
.stale_constant_record.json

# Superseded 2026-08-10 snapshot of an earlier build of the site, kept locally
# during the docs/ migration and referenced by nothing. Ignored so it cannot be
# committed by accident; safe to delete.
web/
